[Event "Stafford Gambit: the gambit itself"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/I6bcI5Em"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/whCVdUeM/1qG3NOtV"]

{ Today we learn the Stafford Gambit. A pawn for fast pieces, and a pile of traps. }
1. e4 { Answer right in the middle. Push your king pawn two squares. [%cal Ye7e5] } 1... e5 (1... c6 { A fine move, and a completely different game. } 2. d4 d5 { That is the Caro-Kann. Today we want the open one. }) 2. Nf3 { He hits your pawn. Do not defend it. Bring the king knight out. [%cal Yg8f6] } 2... Nf6 (2... Nc6 { Just as good, and the engine even prefers it. But it is not our line. } 3. Bb5 a6 { That is the Spanish, and a very long story. }) 3. Nxe5 { The Stafford move. Do not take back. Offer the knight on c6. [%cal Yb8c6] } 3... Nc6 (3... d6 { The sound move, and the engine's favourite. But that is the quiet Petrov. } 4. Nf3 Nxe4 5. d4 { Even, correct, and nobody falls for anything. }) (3... Nxe4 { Also perfectly good and perfectly calm. } 4. Qe2 Qe7 5. Qxe4 d6 { The queens come off. Sound, but not our adventure. }) 4. Nxc6 { He takes. Now recapture with the d pawn, not the b pawn. [%cal Yd7c6] } 4... dxc6 { The Stafford. Bishop and queen are free and the d file is open. [%csl Gc8,Gd8] } (4... bxc6 { Just as good by the numbers. But it walls your bishop in. [%csl Rc8] } 5. Nc3 Bb4 { Playable, and far less fun. }) 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: oh no, my queen"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/kZFfzTvW"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_0_5"]

{ Here is the Stafford position again. He has a pawn; you have quicker pieces. }
5. d3 { He props the pawn up. Put your bishop where it stares at his weak square. [%cal Yf8c5] } 5... Bc5 { The bishop takes aim at f2, the square his king still guards alone. [%cal Gc5f2] } (5... Be6 { The engine's own pick, and perfectly solid. But we want the bishop hitting f2. } 6. Be2 Qe7 7. f4 { A sound game, and a much quieter one. }) (5... Qe7 { Also good, and it eyes the open file. But the bishop belongs on c5. } 6. Nc3 Be6 { Perfectly playable, with no trap in sight. }) 6. Bg5 { He pins your knight to the queen. Look again: is it really pinned? [%csl Rf6] } 6... Nxe4 { It never was. Now his bishop and your queen both hang. [%cal Ge4g5,Ge4f2] } (6... Bxf2+ { Also winning, and very tempting. } 7. Kxf2 Nxe4+ 8. Ke1 Nxg5 { A pawn and a bishop. Good. The queen was bigger. }) 7. Bxd8 { He took the queen, and that was his mistake. Check with the bishop. [%cal Yc5f2] } 7... Bxf2+ (7... Kxd8 { The natural take back, and it throws everything away. } 8. dxe4 Bd6 { You are simply a queen down. The check came first. }) (7... Nxf2 { The wrong piece. Now he has time. } 8. Qh5 b6 9. Qg5 { His queen escapes and you are lost. }) 8. Ke2 { Forced. Now bring the last piece out and finish it. [%cal Yc8g4] } 8... Bg4# (8... Bb6 { The bishop steps back to safety, and the mate is gone. } 9. h3 Ng3+ 10. Kf3 { He is a whole queen up and you had mate in one. }) 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: oh no, my knight"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/qiKryhNu"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_0_5"]

{ This time he pushes the pawn past your knight. Where does the knight go? }
5. e5 { The pawn chases your knight. Jump forward, into the middle of his camp. [%cal Yf6e4] } 5... Ne4 { Straight to e4. Be honest: the engine likes d5 better, but e4 sets the trap. [%csl Ge4] } (5... Nd5 { The engine's choice, and objectively the best move on the board. } 6. d4 c5 7. c3 { A sound position for you. But no trap, and no fun. }) (5... Ng8 { All the way home. Safe, and you have thrown away two moves. } 6. Bc4 Qd4 { Playable, but he is simply better. }) 6. d3 { The natural kick. Your knight is attacked. Do not move it. [%csl Re4] } 6... Bc5 { Ignore the knight. The bishop joins the attack on f2. [%cal Gc5f2] } (6... Nc5 { The safe retreat, and White is simply better after it. } 7. d4 Nd7 { You saved the knight and lost the whole idea. }) 7. dxe4 { He grabbed the knight, and that was the blunder. Check with the bishop. [%cal Yc5f2] } 7... Bxf2+ (7... Qxd1+ { Taking the queen first, and it throws the whole thing away. } 8. Kxd1 Bxf2 9. Nd2 { You are simply a piece down. }) (7... Qh4 { It looks like a check, but it is not one. } 8. Qf3 Bd4 { He gets organised and you have nothing. }) 8. Kxf2 { The king must take, and now nobody is guarding his queen. [%cal Yd8d1] } 8... Qxd1 { Oh no, his queen. A knight given, a queen won. } (8... Qh4+ { A check, but it lets his king run and saves the queen. } 9. Kg1 Qxe4 10. Qe2 { A pawn instead of a queen. }) 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: he does not take the knight"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
