[FEN "r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p5/2b1P3/4n3/3P4/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 1 7"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p5/2b1P3/4n3/3P4/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_1_7"]

{ Same position, but this time he sees the trap and does not touch your knight. }
7. Be3 { His best try: trade off the bishop that is aiming at f2. Take it. [%cal Yc5e3] } 7... Bxe3 (7... Nxf2 { A wild sacrifice, but he just takes it. } 8. Kxf2 Bxe3+ 9. Kxe3 { Two pieces for nothing. }) 8. fxe3 { His king is bare on the f file now. Bring the queen with check. [%cal Yd8h4] } 8... Qh4+ (8... Nc5 { The knight finally moves, and the moment passes. } 9. Qh5 Qe7 { White is better again. The check was the point. }) 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: take the knight, lose the rook"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/OPZzl8KT"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p5/4P3/4n3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 1 6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p5/4P3/4n3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_1_6"]

{ Your knight is on e4 again, the way you put it there last time. He builds a centre. }
6. d4 { He grabs space instead of kicking you. Now the queen has a bold square. [%cal Yd8h4] } 6... Qh4 { The queen swings out and stares at f2. Nothing can chase her away. [%cal Gh4f2] } (6... Be6 { The engine likes this just as much. A calm developing move. } 7. c3 f5 { Solid, but the queen check is what sets the trap. }) (6... f5 { You prop the knight up with a pawn. Reasonable, and slow. } 7. Be2 Be7 { He gets to castle and you have no attack. }) 7. g3 { He blocks the check and attacks your queen. Give the knight away. [%csl Rh4] } 7... Nxg3 { A knight for a pawn, and his king has no cover left. [%cal Gh4h1] } (7... Qh3 { The queen steps aside and keeps watching. But nothing follows. } 8. Bg2 Nc5 { White is fine and the moment has gone. }) 8. fxg3 { He takes back. Now the fork, and the rook drops. [%cal Yh4e4] } 8... Qe4+ (8... Bb4+ { A check first looks clever, but it lets him tidy up. } 9. Kf2 Qe4 10. Bg2 { He has castled by hand and holds on. }) 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: the most common trap"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/rRL32xpq"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_0_5"]

{ He develops the knight this time. Your bishop still wants the same diagonal. }
5. Nc3 { A normal developing move. Point your bishop at f2 again. [%cal Yf8c5] } 5... Bc5 { The bishop comes to c5, where it stares straight through to f2. [%cal Gc5f2] } (5... Be6 { A good square for the bishop, but c5 is where it bites. } 6. d3 Qe7 { Playable, and completely harmless. }) (5... Qe7 { Also fine. But the bishop is your best attacker here. } 6. d3 Be6 7. Be2 { You will want the bishop on c5 anyway. }) 6. d3 { The most natural move on the board, and the most common mistake. } 6... Ng4 { Two pieces hit f2 at once. He cannot guard it twice. [%cal Gg4f2,Gc5f2] } (6... O-O { Sensible, and White is simply a pawn up. } 7. Be2 Ng4 8. Bxg4 { He trades the knight off and you have nothing. }) 7. Be3 { His only real defence. Take with the knight, not the bishop. [%cal Yg4e3] } 7... Nxe3 (7... Bxe3 { The other capture. It looks the same and it is not. } 8. fxe3 Nxe3 9. Qd2 { His queen collects the knight and he is fine. }) 8. fxe3 { Now take the pawn back. His king will never castle. [%cal Yc5e3] } 8... Bxe3 (8... Qg5 { Also strong, and worth about the same. } 9. Qf3 Bxe3 { The same idea, one move later. }) (8... Qh4+ { A check first, also about equal. } 9. g3 Qg5 { Fine too. Taking on e3 is cleaner. }) 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: the knight comes back"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
