[FEN "r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/2b5/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 2 6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/2b5/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_2_6"]

{ Same set up as last time: his knight out, your bishop on c5 aiming at f2. }
6. Bc4 { He develops at you instead of playing d3. The jump to g4 still works. [%cal Yf6g4] } 6... Ng4 { Both your pieces hit f2 again, and this time his bishop is loose too. [%cal Gg4f2,Gc5f2] } (6... O-O { Safe and sensible, and the moment quietly passes. } 7. f3 b5 8. Bb3 { He is a pawn up and consolidating. }) (6... Bxf2+ { The greedy check. He steps across and you have given a bishop. } 7. Kxf2 Qd4+ 8. Ke1 { A bishop for one pawn. }) 7. Qf3 { The queen guards f2 and eyes f7. Your knight is loose. Where to? [%csl Rg4] } 7... Ne5 { Back it comes, hitting the queen, the bishop and f7 at once. [%cal Ge5f3,Ge5c4,Ge5f7] } (7... O-O { Safe, and he is only a little worse. } 8. h3 Nf6 { The knight goes home and the fun is over. }) (7... Bxf2+ { Tempting, but he simply steps across. } 8. Ke2 O-O { A bishop for one pawn. }) 8. Qe2 { The queen steps aside. Bring your own queen out and pile in. [%cal Yd8h4] } 8... Qh4 (8... a5 { A useful little move, and nearly as good. } 9. Qf1 b5 { You keep an edge, but the queen was faster. }) 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: the quiet moves are not safe"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/Ibwl11lm"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/2b5/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 2 6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/2b5/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_2_6"]

{ He tucks the bishop away quietly this time and thinks he is out of the woods. }
6. Be2 { A safe square, and no target. Throw the h pawn up the board at him. [%cal Yh7h5] } 6... h5 { The rook pawn walks, opening a road for the rook behind it. [%cal Gh8h5] } (6... Qd4 { Even stronger, and the engine's first choice: you can play it at once. } 7. O-O Nxe4 8. Bf3 { Also very good. But we want him to play h3 first. }) (6... Be6 { A normal developing move, and he is simply better. } 7. d3 Qd6 { Nothing is happening and he keeps the pawn. }) 7. h3 { Stopping your knight from g4. That frees a square for the queen. [%csl Gd4] } 7... Qd4 { Right into the middle. She hits e4 and f2 and cannot be chased off. [%cal Gd4e4,Gd4f2] } (7... Bd4 { The bishop wants the same square. But the queen belongs there. } 8. d3 Qe7 { Solid, and he keeps his extra pawn. }) (7... h4 { Pushing on. Far too slow, he just develops. } 8. d3 Be6 { Nothing is happening yet. }) 8. Rf1 { His one saving move. Play d3 or f3 there and you mate him. [%csl Rf2] } 8... Nxe4 { The knight joins in and he is very uncomfortable. } (8... Bb4 { Pinning the knight, but it lets him breathe. } 9. d3 Bxc3+ 10. bxc3 { He unwinds and stands better. }) 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: everyone falls for this one"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/tWZBMLsI"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/2b5/4P3/3P4/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 1 6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/2b5/4P3/3P4/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_1_6"]

{ Back to the d3 line, bishop already on c5. Now the whole king side comes at him. }
6. Be2 { He hides the bishop. Send the rook pawn forward: this one is our trap. [%cal Yh7h5] } 6... h5 { The engine prefers the knight to g4. We push the pawn, because he will grab it. [%cal Gh8h5] } (6... Ng4 { The engine's pick, and a good move. But the h pawn is our idea. } 7. Bxg4 Qh4 8. Qf3 { You win the pawn back, and there is no attack. }) (6... a6 { A slow pawn move while you are a pawn down. } 7. c3 Qe7 { He is comfortable and you have nothing. }) 7. O-O { He castles into it. Now the knight jumps, and the pawn is bait. [%cal Yf6g4] } 7... Ng4 { The knight offers itself on g4, right in front of the open h file. [%cal Gh5g4] } (7... h4 { Pushing on without the knight. Far too slow. } 8. c3 Be6 { He has time to organise and you are worse. }) 8. h3 { He asks the question. Do not move the knight: bring the queen instead. [%cal Yd8d6] } 8... Qd6 { Queen, bishop, knight and rook now all point at his king. [%cal Gd6h2] } (8... Ne5 { You save the knight, and the whole attack disappears. } 9. Qd2 Be6 { A pawn down with nothing to show. }) 9. hxg4 { He took, and that was the mistake. Take back with the h pawn. [%cal Yh5g4] } 9... hxg4 { The h file is open and there is no defence to the mate. [%cal Gd6h2] } (9... Bxg4 { The natural recapture, and it throws the whole attack away. } 10. Nd2 O-O-O 11. Nc4 { He trades pieces off and stands much better. }) 10. e5 { A desperate block. Take it. [%cal Yd6e5] } 10... Qxe5 (10... Bxf2+ { Also crushing, but the queen was already on her way. } 11. Rxf2 Qxe5 { The same place, one move slower. }) 11. Bh6 { He throws the bishop in the way. Take it with the rook. [%cal Yh8h6] } 11... Rxh6 (11... gxh6 { The pawn takes, and the whole win vanishes. } 12. Bxg4 Be6 13. Bxe6 { Your rook never gets to the h file. The position is level. }) 12. Re1 { Nothing helps him now. Check on h2. [%cal Ye5h2] } 12... Qh2+ { And it is mate next move on h1. } (12... Bxf2+ { This mates too, just more slowly. } 13. Kf1 Qe3 { Also winning. The check on h2 was quicker. }) 0-1
